# Inventory Project Conventions

## Stack

- Next.js 16 App Router, React, TypeScript, Tailwind CSS
- Bun for package management (`bun install`, `bun dev`, `bun run build`)
- Supabase for database and auth (`@supabase/ssr`, `@supabase/supabase-js`)

## Folder structure

All application code lives under `src/`:

```
src/
├── app/          # Pages, layouts, and API route handlers
│   └── api/      # Backend — one folder per route, export GET/POST/etc.
├── component/    # Shared UI components (singular folder name)
├── lib/          # Utilities and integrations (e.g. supabase/)
└── types/        # Shared TypeScript types; import via @/types
```

- Use `@/` path aliases — never deep relative imports like `../../`
- Do not add code outside `src/` except config files (`proxy.ts`, `next.config.ts`, etc.)

## Supabase

- Browser client: `@/lib/supabase/client` — Client Components only
- Server client: `@/lib/supabase/server` — Server Components, Server Actions, API routes
- Session refresh: `proxy.ts` at project root (Next.js 16; not `middleware.ts`)
- Env vars: `NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL`, `NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY`
- Never expose `service_role` or secret keys in client code
- Use `supabase.auth.getUser()` for auth checks, not `getSession()`
- Cookie handling: only `getAll` / `setAll` — never `get`, `set`, or `remove`

## API routes

- Place handlers in `src/app/api/<name>/route.ts`
- Export named HTTP methods: `GET`, `POST`, `PUT`, `DELETE`
- Return `NextResponse.json()` for JSON responses
- Use the server Supabase client for database access

## General

- Keep diffs minimal and focused
- Match existing naming and patterns in surrounding code
- Do not commit `.env.local` or secrets
